window.docContents[235].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-hyperoctahedral-group":{"contents":"We can now describe the translations and coordinate permutations as a single group:\nthe semidirect product of (ℤ/2)ⁿ (as Fin n → Bool under pointwise xor) by the\nsymmetric group Sₙ, where a permutation acts on a translation vector by permuting\nits coordinates. This is exactly the wreath product ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ, known as the\nhyperoctahedral group.\n\nMathlib's SemidirectProduct N G φ (notation N ⋊[φ] G) is stated for\nmultiplicative groups, so we need to wrap our additive group Fin n → Bool in\nMultiplicative. This is a type synonym: Multiplicative α has the same\nelements as α, but its group operation * is the + of α. We pass between the\ntwo with the identity maps Multiplicative.ofAdd and Multiplicative.toAdd, and\nturn an additive automorphism into a multiplicative one with\nAddEquiv.toMultiplicative. The action φ sends σ to the group\nautomorphism t ↦ t ∘ σ.symm of Fin n → Bool, which Mathlib provides as\nAddEquiv.arrowCongr σ (AddEquiv.refl Bool).\n\n/-- The action of Equiv.Perm (Fin n) on translation vectors by permuting\ncoordinates, as a MonoidHom into the automorphisms of\nMultiplicative (Fin n → Bool). -/\ndef hammingPhi (n : ℕ) :\n    Equiv.Perm (Fin n) →* MulAut (Multiplicative (Fin n → Bool)) where\n  toFun σ := AddEquiv.toMultiplicative (AddEquiv.arrowCongr σ (AddEquiv.refl Bool))\n  map_one' := by ext t; rfl\n  map_mul' σ ρ  := by ext t; rfl\n\n\n/-- The hyperoctahedral group (ℤ/2)ⁿ ⋊ Sₙ, i.e. the wreath product ℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ,\nrealized as a semidirect product with (ℤ/2)ⁿ as Fin n → Bool under pointwise\nxor. -/\nabbrev hyperoctahedralGroup (n : ℕ) : Type :=\n  Multiplicative (Fin n → Bool) ⋊[hammingPhi n] Equiv.Perm (Fin n)\n\n\nTranslations and coordinate permutations give monoid homomorphisms into the\nautomorphism group of the Hamming graph. (Recall that multiplication of automorphisms\nis composition, (f * g) x = f (g x).)\n\n/-- Translations as a monoid homomorphism into the automorphism group of the Hamming\ngraph. -/\n@[simps]\ndef hammingTranslateHom (n : ℕ) :\n    Multiplicative (Fin n → Bool) →* (hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n) where\n  toFun t := hammingTranslate t.toAdd\n  map_one' := by ext x; simp\n  map_mul' t s := by ext x; simp [add_assoc]\n\n\n/-- Coordinate permutations as a monoid homomorphism into the automorphism group of\nthe Hamming graph. -/\n@[simps]\ndef hammingPermuteHom (n : ℕ) :\n    Equiv.Perm (Fin n) →* (hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n) where\n  toFun := hammingPermute\n  map_one' := by ext x; rfl\n  map_mul' σ ρ := by ext x; rfl\n\n\nThese two homomorphisms are compatible with the action hammingPhi: conjugating a\ntranslation by a coordinate permutation is the translation by the permuted vector.\nSemidirectProduct.lift combines them into a monoid homomorphism from the\nhyperoctahedral group to the automorphism group.\n\n/-- The homomorphism from the hyperoctahedral group to the automorphisms of the\nHamming graph, sending (t, σ) to the automorphism x ↦ t + x ∘ σ.symm, i.e.\ncoordinate permutation by σ followed by translation by t. -/\ndef hyperoctahedralToAut (n : ℕ) :\n    hyperoctahedralGroup n →* (hypercubeHamming n ≃g hypercubeHamming n) :=\n  SemidirectProduct.lift (hammingTranslateHom n) (hammingPermuteHom n) (by\n    intro σ\n    ext t x i\n    change t (σ.symm i) + x i = t (σ.symm i) + x (σ (σ.symm i))\n    rw [Equiv.apply_symm_apply])\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hyperoctahedralToAut_apply {n : ℕ} (t : Multiplicative (Fin n → Bool))\n    (σ : Equiv.Perm (Fin n)) (x : Fin n → Bool) :\n    hyperoctahedralToAut n ⟨t, σ⟩ x = t.toAdd + x ∘ σ.symm := rfl\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009The Automorphism Group of the Hypercube*","header":"12.6.2. The Hyperoctahedral Group","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-hyperoctahedral-group"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-2-simplegraph":{"contents":"* pathNat₁: path graph on ℕ defined using the default SimpleGraph constructor.* pathNat₂: path graph on ℕ defined using SimpleGraph.fromRel.* pathNat₃: path graph on ℕ defined using SimpleGraph.fromEdgeSet.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* shiftedPathNat: graph on ℕ with an edge from n to n + 1 for every n ≥ i.* shortPathNat: graph on ℕ with an edge from n to n + 1 for every n < i.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.1. Chapter 2: SimpleGraph","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-2-simplegraph"},"/Colorings/#section-greedy-colouring-vs-the-crown-graph":{"contents":"The classic example of a graph on which greedy colouring performs badly is the\nbalanced complete bipartite graph with the obvious perfect matching removed, known as\nthe crown graph, with its vertices ordered left, right, left, right, ...\n\nWe will look at its countably infinite incarnation. We will show that the greedy\ncolouring will use arbitrarily large colours, despite the graph being 2-colourable.\n\nGreedy vs. optimal colouring of crownGraph\n\n/-- The *crown graph* is the complete bipartite graph on Even and Odd naturals,\nminus the perfect matching 2 * n ↦ 2 * n + 1. -/\n@[simps]\ndef crownGraph : SimpleGraph ℕ where\n  Adj a b := a % 2 ≠ b % 2 ∧ a / 2 ≠ b / 2\n\n\ninstance : DecidableRel crownGraph.Adj := by\n  unfold crownGraph; infer_instance\n\n\nThe earlier neighbours of n in crownGraph are {1, 3, ..., n - 1}, if n is\neven, and {0, 2, ..., n - 3} if n is odd. We can see this in examples via #eval\nand will prove it below.\n\n#eval crownGraph.neighborLTFinset 6 -- {1, 3, 5}\n#eval crownGraph.neighborLTFinset 11 -- {0, 2, 4, 6, 8}\n\n\nlemma crownGraph_neighborLTFinset (n : ℕ) :\n    crownGraph.neighborLTFinset n = (range (n / 2)).image\n        (fun k => if n % 2 = 0 then 2 * k + 1 else 2 * k) := by\n  ext m\n  simp only [mem_neighborLTFinset, crownGraph_adj, mem_image]\n  constructor\n  · intro ⟨hlt, hadj⟩\n    exact ⟨m / 2, by grind, by grind⟩\n  · grind\n\n\n/-- The natural 2-colouring of the crownGraph. -/\ndef crownGraph.twoColoring : crownGraph.Coloring (Fin 2) where\n  toFun n := ⟨n % 2, by grind⟩\n  map_rel' h := by simp_all\n\n\nColouring each vertex by its parity shows that crownGraph is bipartite.\n\nexample : crownGraph.IsBipartite := crownGraph.twoColoring.colorable\n\n\nThe greedy colouring of crownGraph colours vertex n with colour n / 2. Given\nthat degreeLT n = n / 2, this is as bad as it could be! We can see an example via\n#eval, and prove it below.\n\n#eval crownGraph.greedy 11 -- 5\n\n\ntheorem greedy_crownGraph_eq (n : ℕ) :\n    crownGraph.greedy n = n / 2 := by\n  -- we use strong induction on n\n  induction n using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n  | _ n ih =>\n  -- by induction the earlier neighbours of n use exactly the colours < n / 2\n  refine crownGraph.greedy_eq_of_image_eq_range ?_\n  ext c\n  simp only [crownGraph_neighborLTFinset, mem_image, mem_range]\n  grind\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*","header":"7.8.4. Greedy Colouring vs. the Crown Graph","id":"/Colorings/#section-greedy-colouring-vs-the-crown-graph"}});